  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-4,1)$ and passes through the point $(0,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-4,1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4p(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,3),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-1)^{2} & =4p(0+4) \\\\ 4 & =4p(4) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-1)^{2} & =x+4 \\\\ 1 & =x+4 \\\\ 1-4 & =x \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-3.$",
